  "synonym_discrimination": null,
  "usage_note": "The adverb that goes with good is well: she sings well, not she sings good. After feel, look, smell, sound, and taste you use the adjective: I feel good, it tastes good. In American speech I'm good also means no, thank you: More coffee? I'm good, thanks. Good on its own is a reply that shows you are pleased or satisfied.",
  "learner_errors": [
    {
      "incorrect": "She speaks English very good.",
      "correct": "She speaks English very well.",
      "note": "Good is an adjective; the adverb is well."
    },
    {
      "incorrect": "This one is more good.",
      "correct": "This one is better.",
      "note": "The comparative and superlative are better and best, not more good or goodest."
    },
    {
      "incorrect": "I'm good in math.",
      "correct": "I'm good at math.",
      "note": "The preposition after good in the skill sense is at, or with for people and things you handle."
    }
  ],
  "etymology": null,
  "see_also": [],
  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": "Good covers quality, skill, pleasure, kindness, usefulness, and amount; most languages split these across several words, so check the sense before choosing an equivalent. Fixed greetings (good morning) and formulas (good for you) do not translate word for word.",
